- **The constructor changes the options you pass.** It sets `TypeInfoResolver` on them, and throws `InvalidOperationException` when they're read-only. `XmlSerializerOptions.Default`, `Web`, and `Strict` are read-only. If you pass the same options to two contexts, the second one becomes the resolver.
- **Type info belongs to the context's options.** `OrderXmlContext.Default.Order.Options` is the options of `Default`, whichever options instance asked for it.
- **Contexts don't combine through `TypeInfoResolverChain`.** A generated `GetTypeInfo` never returns `null` for a type the reflection resolver can map, so the first context in a chain answers every request.
- **A generated converter applies a subset of the options.** `WriteIndented`, `NewLine`, `OmitXmlDeclaration`, and `DefaultNamespace` apply. `NilHandling`, `DefaultIgnoreCondition`, and converters for class, struct, and built-in member types don't. A `ReferenceHandler` makes the converter throw `NotSupportedException`.
- **Interceptors route other calls to the context.** With the package's build file in effect, `XmlSerializer.Serialize<T>(value, options)` and `XmlSerializer.Deserialize<T>(xml, options)` for a listed type are rewritten to use the context. See [Interceptors](/guides/reflection-vs-source-generation#interceptors).
- **Unlike `JsonSerializerContext`,** the class has no `GeneratedSerializerOptions` member. The generated code is a constructor and properties.
